"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings indicate a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and a pathological T2 stage with positive surgical margins, which are factors suggesting higher risk. Counterbalancing this are the absence of extraprostatic extension, no seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no lymph node metastasis. The biopsy showed a lower grade (Gleason 3+3, ISUP grade group 1) compared to the prostatectomy specimen (Grade group 2). The nodal status is documented as negative (no lymph node metastasis).\n\nThe primary uncertainty lies in the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory, as this information is currently missing. Postoperative PSA levels and their trend are crucial for assessing recurrence risk. Information regarding the postoperative PSA and its trajectory would significantly revise this prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a follow-up period of approximately 57 months without an event. The fixed estimated horizon is 56.82 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 45.45–68.18 months; its coverage has not been validated."
